Paul Lenz wrote:

> "Moonsilence" <usenetNO@JUNKmoonsilence.de> wrote:
> 
>> 1. What is the difference between stealth and invisibility?
> 
> stealth means you walk very quietly. Sleeping monsters won't wake up.

And you'll still be visible; awake monsters will see you. If you're both
stealthy and invisible, you won't be seen *or* heard. 

>> 3. Does it make a diffenece if my pet attacks peaceful/tame monsters?

Not to, for instance, what your god thinks of you. You're not responsible
for your pet's actions.

>> For example, if I play an orcish char, the game promts me wether I
>> really want to attack the o. My pet just does... How can I make him
>> stop doing that? He even attacks watchmen in mine town.
> 
> Pets don't attack tame monsters, but they attack peaceful monsters.

And they don't attack monsters more than two levels higher than
themselves; a large cat will attack a watch captain but not a priest or a
shopkeeper. And they back off when they're wounded (though not always in
time).

> If you are fainting, simply #pray. 

Preferably, just before you're fainting, that is, when you've been Weak
for a while.

>> A shopkeeper angered in an earlier game will remember my name and just
>> start attacking me. Now there is no more point in using the same name.
> 
> IMHO it must have been a bones level. If you died there again and
> the program did not create a bones level again, the shopkeeper will not
> remember your name again.

And if it did create a bones level again, simply play under another name
until the problem's been solved. AFAIK on a Windows system the game
remembers your character name, not your user ID.

>> 8. What's the difference between peaceful and tame monsters? Can I
>> aquire one as a pet?
> 
> Tame monsters ARE pets. You have to tame peaceful monsters to become
> pets.

You can also tame hostile monsters. Domestic cats, dogs and horses
(kitten, housecat and large cat; little dog, dog and large dog; pony,
horse and warhorse) can be tamed with food; most (not all) other
creatures can be tamed with a scroll of taming or a charm monster spell.
You can make hostile domestic creatures peaceful by throwing them food
they can't eat, like a tin.

